<onlyinclude>An '''inforg''' (informational organism) is a philosophical concept developed by Luciano Floridi to describe an entity, especially a human being, understood through its informational relationships with other entities and its environment. It emphasizes how information and communication technologies shape human identity and activity. It is not a biological classification.

== What the concept describes ==
A person can participate in a connected environment through conversations, medical records, navigation services, financial transactions and relationships. These interactions can affect the person's choices and opportunities even when no device is physically attached to their body. Floridi uses the inforg idea to examine this connected condition.

== Inforg and infosphere ==
An [[Infosphere/Simple|infosphere]] is the informational environment in which interactions occur. An inforg is an entity understood in relation to that environment. A person and their surrounding communication network are related, but they are not the same concept.

An inforg also differs from a cyborg, which generally involves integration of technology with a biological body. Floridi's account does not require a bodily implant. A software [[Intelligent agent/Simple|agent]] can participate in information exchanges, but that participation alone does not establish consciousness, biological life or a human-like identity.

== Significance and limitations ==
The idea helps frame questions about privacy, responsibility, autonomy and how records or digital relationships affect identity. It is a philosophical interpretation, not a validated measure of intelligence, a clinical diagnosis, or a demonstrated biological mechanism. Philosophers disagree about how far informational descriptions can explain the nature of people and reality.

